---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatusBadges from '../components/StatusBadges.astro';
import { getFeaturedManufacturers, placeLine } from '../lib/manufacturers';

const featured = getFeaturedManufacturers();
---

<Base
  description="A sourced record of American manufacturers. Two separate questions: who owns and runs the company, and where the manufacturing happens."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">A manufacturer record</p>
  <h1>American Made Accountability</h1>
  <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
    <p class="lede">
      This is a public record of manufacturers. Every profile asks two questions, and each answer has to stand on its own: who owns and runs the company, and where the manufacturing happens.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The pages are written for adults. The tone can be warm. The claims stay tied to sources. When a document is missing, or two accounts disagree, the page says so in plain language.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="two-questions">
    <p class="kicker">The standard</p>
    <h2 id="two-questions">Two badges, read apart</h2>
    <div class="questions" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">01</p>
        <h3>Ownership and control</h3>
        <p>
          Who owns the company, and who runs it day to day. A U.S. plant with a foreign parent takes the foreign-parent result on this badge. Making goods inside the United States does not change the owner.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">02</p>
        <h3>Where the work happens</h3>
        <p>
          Where the manufacturing actually takes place. A company owned in the United States and producing abroad takes the made-abroad result on this badge. That result is recorded as made abroad.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="featured">
    <p class="kicker">On the record</p>
    <h2 id="featured">Featured manufacturer</h2>
    {
      featured.length === 0 ? (
        <p class="prose">No featured profile is published yet.</p>
      ) : (
        <div class="card-grid" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
          {featured.map((profile) => (
            <a class="card" href={`/manufacturers/${profile.slug}`}>
              <p class="eyebrow">{profile.category}</p>
              <h2>{profile.name}</h2>
              <p class="quiet" style="margin:0">
                {profile.legalName} · {placeLine(profile)}
              </p>
              <StatusBadges ownership={profile.ownership} manufacturing={profile.manufacturing} />
              <p class="deck" style="margin:0">
                {profile.deck}
              </p>
              <p style="margin:0;font-weight:700">Read the full profile</p>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      )
    }
    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <a href="/manufacturers">Go to the manufacturer index</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="how">
    <p class="kicker">How a name gets here</p>
    <h2 id="how">Files in the repository, not a shopping directory</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Each manufacturer is a JSON file in this site’s repository. The published page is that file, rendered. Profiles are not stored in Netlify Blobs, and they are not pulled from a live catalog.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Made-in-USA directories are lead lists. A name found there can be worth a look. It is not a profile, and this site does not copy a directory’s layout, filters, or product grids.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        A profile stays on the manufacturer: the company, the plant, the owners, the dated changes, and the sources. Patterns and prices stay on the company’s own website.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
